"""tools/vtable-layout -- SourceSharp.Abi's vtable structs, generated from the
interface headers.

    tools/vtable-layout               regenerate and DIFF against the committed
                                      VtableLayouts.g.cs
    tools/vtable-layout --emit        print the generated file
    tools/vtable-layout --write       write it
    tools/vtable-layout --self-test   check this script's own reporting

    exit 0   the committed file is what the headers say
    exit 1   it is not -- run --write and read the diff
    exit 2   a header could not be read, or a type could not be mapped

WHAT THIS REPLACED, AND WHY IT IS SO MUCH SMALLER
=================================================

There used to be `tools/vtable-index`, and beside it `tools/vtable-live-diff`,
`EngineVtableDifferential`, `Rtti`, a conditional-floor measurement against the
shipped engine, and a ledger per interface pairing every
slot with a mangled symbol and a refusal. Five derivations of every index,
compared, plus a live differential that called each bound method and held the
answer against something outside the engine.

All of it is gone, on the owner's ruling, and the ruling is one sentence:

    THE ENGINE FACTORY INTERFACES ARE STABLE AND DO NOT CHANGE UNLESS THE
    INTERFACE VERSION STRING CHANGES.

That is the contract Valve's own convention already states, and it makes the
elaborate half of the old apparatus an answer to a question nobody was asking.
`CreateInterface` either answers for "VEngineClient014" or it does not, and if
it does, the layout is the layout that string names.

WHAT IS LEFT, AND IT IS ONE COMPARISON
======================================

A generated struct is a CLAIM about how many methods an interface has, and the
only way that claim can be wrong is if this file goes stale against the header.
So:

  * native holds a precompiled count per version string, taken from the
    compiler via `VTableHook::MemberOf( &Interface::LastMethod ).slot + 1`
    (src/sourcesharp/game/shared/shell_vtable_interfaces.cpp);
  * `SourceSharp_AcquireInterface` hands managed the pointer AND that count;
  * managed compares it to `sizeof(TVtbl) / sizeof(void *)` and refuses,
    naming both numbers, when they differ.

Nothing per call. Nothing at all once the object is bound.

AND THE ASSUMPTION UNDER IT IS MADE TRUE RATHER THAN TRUSTED. Native is
compiled with the same defines as the engine -- which for `cdll_int.h` means
`USE_SDL` in and `NO_VOICE` out, the shipped engine's own settings. That is
stated at the top of every translation unit that compiles those headers and
pinned by a static_assert in
src/sourcesharp/test/vtable_index_agreement_test.cpp.

EVERY SLOT IS TYPED, AND THAT IS A CHANGE
=========================================

The old file carried `nint NotBound_<Method>` for 259 of its 350 fields --
"nobody has looked". Nobody was ever going to look, because looking meant
re-running a five-derivation apparatus that no longer exists. The full C++
declaration was already printed in the trailing comment of every one of them,
so the types were present and unused.

They are used now. Every slot gets a real `delegate* unmanaged<...>`, derived
from the header's own parameter list AND its return type. THE STRUCT DESCRIBES
THE ABI; it does not decide what may be called.

REFUSAL IS A NARROWER WORD HERE THAN IT WAS
===========================================

`Refused_` now means exactly one thing: MANAGED CANNOT EXPRESS THE CALL. Seven
slots of 350 -- five varargs and two returning `Vector` by value.

FOUR of the seven have a C++ proxy taking marshalable parameters, named in the
field's own comment: IVEngineClient013's two `Con_N*Printf` and its two
`GetLightForPoint*`. The other three are IVEngineServer's varargs, and they get
no proxy on purpose -- policy already exposes nothing there, so a proxy would be
dead code. `ClientCommand` says so itself: "varargs ... and it runs a command on
a client, so it would be refused twice over".

Everything else the old file called a refusal was POLICY: a side effect, an
environment-dependent agreement, zero call sites. Policy decides what the IMPL
CLASS exposes, never what the struct says, and its 73 reasons are carried in
tools/vtable-policy.json and copied onto the fields they name. A refusal whose
only trace is a deleted method is one somebody re-attempts.

The 73rd was found by this rule rather than carried into it: IVEngineServer's
`Con_NXPrintf` had no reason of its own, only a mention inside `Con_NPrintf`'s
prose, where nothing keyed by method could see it.

THE DESTRUCTOR PAIR
===================

An interface with a virtual destructor reserves its FIRST TWO vtable slots, and
this is emitted uniformly for any such interface rather than as a special case
for IVModelInfo:

  Complete Object Destructor (D1) -- destroys the object's members and
  subobjects but does not free the memory. Used for objects on the stack, for
  member variables, and for elements in an array.

  Deleting Destructor (D0) -- calls the complete object destructor (D1) and
  then invokes operator delete to free the dynamic memory. Used when you
  explicitly call `delete ptr`.

They are commented "destructor 1" and "destructor 2", are never bound, and must
never be called from managed: slot 1 would free the engine's singleton.
"""

